// Regression test: D7 (docs/development/changes/TYPE-002.md, S15) -- the
// plan's own testing scope (ARCHITECTURE_OVERHAUL.md §8's TYPE-002 entry)
// named `fail_generic_requires_operator.tr`, deferred twice (S12a -> S13a/
// S13b) on the grounds it "is not exercisable yet". That reasoning went
// stale once struct-bound generics were monomorphized (S13a/S13b): a `T`
// lacking an operation its generic body needs is now a real, reachable
// instantiation-site error.
//
// `Set<T>` requires `T` to support `==` (`Set.indexOf()`'s own
// `self.items[i] == item`) -- a plain struct doesn't, by design (no
// trait/constraint system, F-GEN-3 frozen; struct `==` requires an
// explicit `operator ==` overload, tests/18_operator_overload/
// 02_struct_operator_eq.tr). `s.add(...)` on a `Set<Big>` (no operator==
// overload for Big) must be rejected, not silently miscompiled.
//
// KNOWN, DISCLOSED GAP (not asserted here -- see
// ARCHITECTURE_OVERHAUL.md §27.2 item D25 and TYPE-002.md's own D7 entry):
// the plan's stated requirement is that this error "points at the
// instantiation site" -- today it does not. The primary diagnostic anchors
// at `lib/std/collections/set.tr`'s own `Set.contains()` (the innermost
// REQUESTER of `Set.indexOf<Big>`, itself called by `Set.add()`), one hop
// short of naming this file's own `s.add(...)` call that actually started
// the chain. `InstantiationRequest` tracks no parent pointer to walk
// backward through, so there is no chain to anchor at the true outermost
// site with. Fixing this is a real feature addition (also fixes the E079
// chain-noise issue TYPE-002.md's own *Known pitfalls* already discloses),
// deliberately not bundled into this defect-fix session -- this test
// asserts only that the mechanism correctly REJECTS the program, which the
// runner's `fail_*.tr` convention already covers (a reported diagnostic,
// not a hang or an internal-compiler-error).
import set::Set;

struct Big { a: int, b: int, c: int }
fn Big.new(x: int, y: int, z: int): void { self.a = x; self.b = y; self.c = z; }

fn main(): int {
    mut s: Set<Big> = Set::default();
    s.add(Big::new(1, 2, 3));
    return 0;
}
